{"causes": ["The Roman Empire's vast territorial expansion necessitated the recruitment of auxiliary soldiers from diverse provinces like Phoenicia.", "The discovery of a Roman tombstone in 1859 in Bingerbrück, Germany, revealed the specific identity and military service of Pantera.", "Early Jewish-Christian polemics, particularly Celsus's 2nd-century writings, transmitted an accusation linking Jesus's paternity to a soldier named Pantera."], "effects": ["Pantera's name became a focal point in historical-critical debates concerning the historicity and origins of Jesus of Nazareth.", "The discovery of his tombstone provided concrete archaeological evidence for Roman auxiliary presence and multi-ethnic composition along the Rhine frontier.", "His story continues to inspire scholarly inquiry into the social history of the Roman army and the complex interplay of historical evidence and religious narrative."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "roman-empire", "sourceName": "Roman Empire", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "tiberius-julius-abdes-pantera", "targetName": "Tiberius Julius Abdes Pantera", "context": "Tiberius Julius Abdes Pantera served as an auxiliary soldier within the vast military structure of the Roman Empire during the 1st century CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "tiberius-julius-abdes-pantera", "sourceName": "Tiberius Julius Abdes Pantera", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "bingerbruck-germany", "targetName": "Bingerbrück, Germany", "context": "Pantera's tombstone was discovered in Bingerbrück, Germany, in 1859, revealing details of his military service and place of death."}, {"sourceSlug": "celsus", "sourceName": "Celsus", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "tiberius-julius-abdes-pantera", "targetName": "Tiberius Julius Abdes Pantera", "context": "The 2nd-century philosopher Celsus transmitted an accusation that Jesus's biological father was a Roman soldier named Pantera in his work *The True Word*."}, {"sourceSlug": "tiberius-julius-abdes-pantera", "sourceName