": "Tiberius Julius Abdes Pantera was a Roman-Phoenician soldier born in Sidon, whose tombstone was found by railworkers in Bingerbrück, Germany, in 1859.", "newValue": "Tiberius Julius Abdes Pantera, a Roman-Phoenician auxiliary soldier who died around 40 CE, is a figure of enduring historical intrigue, primarily due to a controversial connection posited by early anti-Christian polemicists. Born in Sidon, Phoenicia, his identity as a Roman citizen and veteran of Cohors I Augusta Praetoria Lusitanorum was concretely established by the discovery of his tombstone in 1859, providing a rare glimpse into the lives of non-Roman soldiers serving the Empire during the early principate.

Pantera enlisted in the Roman army, serving 40 years before his death in Bingerbrück (modern-day Germany), likely while stationed with his cohort in Germania Inferior. His tombstone, found by railworkers, specifies his unit, origin, and service length, offering valuable epigraphic evidence of Roman military logistics and multi-ethnic recruitment. This individual record helps reconstruct the diverse composition of the Roman legions and auxiliary forces, highlighting the integration of provincial subjects into the imperial military structure from the Levant to the Rhine frontier.

